24
00:01:46,677 --> 00:01:52,015
Each requires a mechanism. Prediction:
20 points, four items, no terminal.

25
00:01:52,055 --> 00:01:58,119
Hands-on diagnosis: 30 points. Oral interview:
20 points, six questions asked one at

26
00:01:58,159 --> 00:02:01,970
a time, each with a follow-up,
spoken, without notes.

27
00:02:02,310 --> 00:02:08,864
The pass rule. The threshold overall, and
at least 70 percent in every part: 21 of

28
00:02:08,904 --> 00:02:15,216
30 in Concepts, 14 of 20 in Prediction,
21 of 30 in Hands-on, 14 of 20 in Oral.

29
00:02:15,556 --> 00:02:20,981
Quick quiz. Your total is 88, and
your Prediction part is 13 of 20.

30
00:02:21,021 --> 00:02:23,754
A, a pass. B, a miss. Your answer?

31
00:02:28,594 --> 00:02:34,461
B, a miss. A total above the threshold with
one part below 70 percent is a miss, and

32
00:02:34,501 --> 00:02:35,868
Prediction needs 14.

33
00:02:36,208 --> 00:02:41,492
What is different in Part 3. In gates 1 to
5 a script built a broken repository and

34
00:02:41,532 --> 00:02:46,882
you repaired it in the lab shell. Gates 6
to 8 can't work that way, because GitHub's

35
00:02:46,922 --> 00:02:52,271
behavior can't be captured in the lab. So
Part 3 is three cases on paper, built from

36
00:02:52,311 --> 00:02:56,377
configuration files, described
situations and real Git evidence.

37
00:02:56,717 --> 00:03:02,340
The rule reads: these gates run nothing on
GitHub. No GitHub output appears anywhere

38
00:03:02,380 --> 00:03:07,532
in them. And the only gh invocation you
need is gh with a command and --help.

39
00:03:07,572 --> 00:03:11,577
For the paper cases the gate
file gives the points per case.

40
00:03:11,917 --> 00:03:16,228
The layer rule. An answer that doesn't
say which layer acted loses points.

41
00:03:16,268 --> 00:03:19,579
This is the habit the whole
of Part 5 was meant to build.

42
00:03:19,619 --> 00:03:23,694
"The branch is behind" is a Git fact.
You can prove it with a command.

43
00:03:23,734 --> 00:03:28,574
"The merge is blocked because the branch
is behind" is a GitHub decision, made by a

44
00:03:28,614 --> 00:03:32,689
rule that someone configured. Say
both halves, and say which is which.

45
00:03:34,529 --> 00:03:37,192
Use one form for every case: two columns.

46
00:03:37,532 --> 00:03:42,300
On the left, "Git says". Everything here
is something a clone can show: ancestry,

47
00:03:42,340 --> 00:03:47,286
the merge base, the commits a pull request
introduces, the paths it changes, whether

48
00:03:47,326 --> 00:03:52,035
a commit has a signature header, which
version of a file exists on which branch.

49
00:03:52,375 --> 00:03:56,791
On the right, "GitHub says". Everything
here is platform state or a platform

50
00:03:56,831 --> 00:04:01,422
decision: which rules apply, the review
decision, which checks are required and

51
00:04:01,462 --> 00:04:06,347
whether a result exists for the newest
commit, which CODEOWNERS file is read, what a

52
00:04:06,387 --> 00:04:09,161
badge displays, which
merge methods are allowed.

53
00:04:09,501 --> 00:04:14,527
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Two
columns, four facts: the merge base, the

54
00:04:14,567 --> 00:04:17,987
review decision, the changed
paths, the required checks.

55
00:04:20,327 --> 00:04:24,793
The merge base and the changed paths
go on the left: a clone can show them.

56
00:04:24,833 --> 00:04:29,358
The review decision and the required
checks go on the right: platform state.

57
00:04:29,698 --> 00:04:34,046
Then the finding: one sentence that connects
a line on the left to a line on the

58
00:04:34,086 --> 00:04:34,375
right.

59
00:04:34,715 --> 00:04:39,190
Where this form falls short: some facts
need both columns to state at all.

60
00:04:39,230 --> 00:04:44,193
"Stale approval" is a platform concept
defined by a Git event, a push that changed

61
00:04:44,233 --> 00:04:47,426
the diff. Write those across
both columns and say so.

62
00:04:47,766 --> 00:04:53,005
Three procedures from this part give you
the rows. The authentication decision tree,

63
00:04:53,045 --> 00:04:57,341
for any case that starts with a
refused connection or a refused push.

64
00:04:57,381 --> 00:05:01,992
The anatomy of a pull request, for any
case about unexpected commits or an

65
00:05:02,032 --> 00:05:06,642
unexpected diff. And the nine steps of
"why can't I merge" from video 134.

66
00:05:08,482 --> 00:05:13,539
Here's the opening sentence, answered. The
finding joins one line from each column:

67
00:05:13,579 --> 00:05:17,469
the strict policy, GitHub, is
unmet because the base moved, Git.

68
00:05:19,309 --> 00:05:21,050
Step 1: the commit list.

69
00:05:21,390 --> 00:05:25,014
Which tab of a pull request
does this range correspond to?

70
00:05:25,054 --> 00:05:26,025
Say it out loud.

71
00:05:30,865 --> 00:05:35,876
The Commits tab: the commits reachable
from the head branch and not from the base

72
00:05:35,916 --> 00:05:38,807
branch. Three of them,
with 16d4788 at the tip.

73
00:05:39,147 --> 00:05:41,111
Step 2: the merge base.

74
00:05:41,451 --> 00:05:46,566
9a383e5, "Add classifier test". Write
the merge base down in every case that

75
00:05:46,606 --> 00:05:52,196
involves a diff. Half the surprises of
Chapter 17 are a merge base that isn't where

76
00:05:52,236 --> 00:05:53,824
the author thinks it is.

77
00:05:54,164 --> 00:05:55,890
Step 3: three dots.

78
00:05:56,230 --> 00:05:59,926
The Files changed tab: the merge
base compared with the head.

79
00:05:59,966 --> 00:06:00,539
Two files.

80
00:06:00,879 --> 00:06:02,346
Step 4: two dots.

81
00:06:05,186 --> 00:06:09,782
main has one commit that the branch doesn't
have. How many files will the two-dot

82
00:06:09,822 --> 00:06:13,617
form list, and in which direction
will that commit's change appear?

83
00:06:13,657 --> 00:06:14,819
Make your prediction.

84
00:06:19,659 --> 00:06:25,141
Three files. config/routing.yaml appears
although the pull request never touched it,

85
00:06:25,181 --> 00:06:30,071
and the change is reversed: the threshold
seems to go from 0.7 back to 0.6.

86
00:06:30,111 --> 00:06:35,331
Two dots compare the two tips. If a case
shows you a diff with a file the author

87
00:06:35,371 --> 00:06:40,327
didn't change, this is your first
question: which two commits were compared?

88
00:06:40,667 --> 00:06:43,412
In the gate, evidence like
this is printed for you.

89
00:06:43,452 --> 00:06:46,469
Your work is to read it and
place it in the left column.

90
00:06:48,309 --> 00:06:49,957
Five mistakes to watch for.

91
00:06:50,297 --> 00:06:54,511
Answering with a repair before naming the
cause. Root cause: the case asks which

92
00:06:54,551 --> 00:06:58,711
rule refused and which fact it looked at,
and a repair without that is a guess.

93
00:06:59,051 --> 00:07:03,979
Not naming the layer. Root cause: Git
computes facts and GitHub applies rules to

94
00:07:04,019 --> 00:07:08,451
them, and an answer that merges the
two cannot say who must change what.

95
00:07:08,791 --> 00:07:12,274
Reading a two-dot diff as "what
the pull request changes".

96
00:07:12,314 --> 00:07:17,133
Root cause: two dots compare the two tips;
the pull request shows the merge base

97
00:07:17,173 --> 00:07:18,531
compared with the head.

98
00:07:18,871 --> 00:07:23,321
Opening the gate file "to see what is in
it". Root cause: the gate measures what you

99
00:07:23,361 --> 00:07:25,887
can do unseen, so reading
it spends the attempt.

100
00:07:26,227 --> 00:07:30,762
Treating the workflow and configuration
files of a paper case as examples to reuse.

101
00:07:30,802 --> 00:07:35,281
Root cause: the files in the gate directories
are teaching material with faults on

102
00:07:35,321 --> 00:07:35,722
purpose.

103
00:07:37,562 --> 00:07:41,542
Now, out of the lab. A backend team's
incident channel, on a Friday.

104
00:07:41,582 --> 00:07:46,391
A developer reports that a hotfix can't be
merged. The engineer on call answers in

105
00:07:46,431 --> 00:07:51,357
the two-column form, in four lines: what
the clone shows, what the rules page shows,

106
00:07:51,397 --> 00:07:54,905
the one rule that's unmet, and
what the repair will trigger.

107
00:07:54,945 --> 00:07:59,044
The developer updates the branch,
waits for the one check, and merges.

108
00:07:59,084 --> 00:08:01,941
Nobody asked an administrator
to bypass anything.

109
00:08:02,281 --> 00:08:06,354
That's what the gate is rehearsing. An
answer in that form can be acted on by

110
00:08:06,394 --> 00:08:08,437
someone who wasn't in the conversation.

111
00:08:10,277 --> 00:08:17,146
